interface.longDescription:
Thalius Hippocampus turns your company's documents into an expert your AI can consult. Behind it is an LLM wiki: at import, language models write every document into folders, documents and sections, each with a summary, an outline and the exact text.

Vector search cuts documents into chunks and mixes them. It is fast on simple lookups but loses the thread on complex questions and large collections. Hippocampus keeps each document whole, so the answer comes from the right document, section and page.

With Hippocampus connected, your AI can:
- answer complex questions across many documents: compare a clause across your contracts, trace a requirement through a specification, check a figure across years of reports;
- quote the source of truth: each passage with its document, section, page and a link that opens it, and say so when the documents do not cover a question;
- work in a small context: it reads the few passages it needs, not whole files; finding, opening and reading are free, searching and importing use credits;
- keep knowledge current: save decisions, meeting notes and new procedures to the workspace when you ask.

Where teams use it: knowledge systems (policies, procedures, handbooks), business processes (offers, claims and onboarding checked against the current rules), research (one question across every paper and report), engineering (specifications, standards and design decisions a coding agent reads before it writes), legal and finance (the clause or the figure with its page).

Your AI acts as you and sees only the documents you may see. Import PDF, scanned PDF, Word, PowerPoint, Excel, Markdown or text, or sync Google Drive and OneDrive. Encrypted in the EU, documents stored in Finland, each with its own key. Not used to train AI models: every provider we use is on terms that exclude training. A free account works; sign-in is OAuth, no key to paste.
